### Examples of Good vs Bad

#### ❌ Bad - Insufficient Documentation

```java
public class TwoSum {
    // Find two numbers that add up to target
    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int complement = target - nums[i];
            if (map.containsKey(complement)) {
                return new int[]{map.get(complement), i};
            }
            map.put(nums[i], i);
        }
        return new int[]{};
    }
}
```

#### ✅ Good - Complete Documentation

```java
package com.javarena.dsa.datastructures.arrays;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * 1. Two Sum
 *
 * <p><b>Problem Link:</b> 
 * <a href="https://leetcode.com/problems/two-sum/">LeetCode - Two Sum</a>
 *
 * <p><b>Difficulty:</b> Easy
 *
 * <p><b>Topics:</b> Array, Hash Table
 *
 * <p><b>Companies:</b> Amazon, Google, Facebook, Microsoft
 *
 * ---
 *
 * <p><b>Problem Statement:</b><br>
 * Given an array of integers nums and an integer target, return indices of the two numbers 
 * such that they add up to target. You may assume that each input would have exactly one 
 * solution, and you may not use the same element twice.
 *
 * <p><b>Example:</b>
 * <pre>
 * Input: nums = [2,7,11,15], target = 9
 * Output: [0,1]
 * Explanation: Because nums[0] + nums[1] == 9, we return [0, 1]
 * </pre>
 *
 * ---
 *
 * <p><b>Intuition:</b><br>
 * The key insight is to use a hash map to store numbers we've seen along with their indices.
 * - For each number, we calculate its complement (target - current number)
 * - If the complement exists in our hash map, we've found our pair
 * - This allows us to solve in a single pass instead of nested loops
 *
 * ---
 *
 * <p><b>Approach:</b>
 * <ol>
 *   <li>Create a hash map to store number → index mapping</li>
 *   <li>Iterate through the array once</li>
 *   <li>For each number, calculate complement = target - current</li>
 *   <li>Check if complement exists in hash map; if yes, return indices</li>
 *   <li>Otherwise, add current number and index to hash map</li>
 * </ol>
 *
 * ---
 *
 * <p><b>Time Complexity:</b> O(n)<br>
 * We traverse the list once, and hash map operations (get/put) are O(1) average case.
 *
 * <p><b>Space Complexity:</b> O(n)<br>
 * In the worst case, we store all n elements in the hash map.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * <p><b>Edge Cases:</b>
 * <ul>
 *   <li>Array with exactly 2 elements: Directly check if they sum to target</li>
 *   <li>Negative numbers: Algorithm works the same way</li>
 *   <li>Zero in array: Handled correctly by complement calculation</li>
 * </ul>
 *
 * @see <a href="https://leetcode.com/problems/two-sum-ii-input-array-is-sorted/">Two Sum II</a>
 */
public class TwoSum {
    
    /**
     * Finds two indices of numbers that add up to the target using hash map.
     *
     * @param nums array of integers to search
     * @param target the target sum to find
     * @return array containing two indices [i, j] where nums[i] + nums[j] == target
     */
    public int[] twoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        // Step 1: Create hash map to store number → index
        Map<Integer, Integer> numToIndex = new HashMap<>();
        
        // Step 2: Iterate through array
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            // Step 3: Calculate complement
            int complement = target - nums[i];
            
            // Step 4: Check if complement exists in our map
            if (numToIndex.containsKey(complement)) {
                return new int[]{numToIndex.get(complement), i};
            }
            
            // Step 5: Add current number to map
            numToIndex.put(nums[i], i);
        }
        
        // Problem guarantees solution exists, but return empty array as fallback
        return new int[]{};
    }
    
    /**
     * Brute force approach - for comparison and learning purposes.
     * 
     * <p><b>Time:</b> O(n²) - nested loops
     * <br><b>Space:</b> O(1) - no extra space needed
     * <br><b>Trade-off:</b> Simple to understand but too slow for large inputs
     *
     * @param nums array of integers
     * @param target target sum
     * @return indices of two numbers that sum to target
     */
    public int[] twoSumBruteForce(int[] nums, int target) {
        // Try all pairs
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {
                if (nums[i] + nums[j] == target) {
                    return new int[]{i, j};
                }
            }
        }
        return new int[]{};
    }
}
```

